      <h1>How to tell someone</h1>
      <p>This page was made by people who have had to find these words themselves. It is peer support, not professional advice. If you might act on what you're feeling, tell someone by calling or texting <a href="tel:988">988</a>, or text TALK to <a href="sms:741741">741741</a> — those count as telling someone, and they are open all night.</p>
      <h2>Why the first sentence is the hardest</h2>
      <p>Most people don't stay silent because they have nothing to say. They stay silent because the first sentence feels like it has to explain everything. It doesn't. The first sentence only has to open the door.</p>
      <h2>Pick the safest door, not the perfect one</h2>
      <p>The right person is not always the closest person. It's the one most likely to listen without fixing, judging, or panicking — a friend, a sibling, a coworker, a pastor, a sponsor. Or a warm line full of strangers who do this every day. Texting is allowed. Writing it down and handing it over is allowed.</p>
      <h2>Borrow a first line</h2>
      <p>Copy one of these. Change what you need to. Sending it as-is is fine too.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>"I'm not okay. I don't need you to fix it — I just need you to know."</li>
        <li>"I've been having a hard time, and I didn't want to carry it alone anymore."</li>
        <li>"Can I tell you something heavy? You don't have to say the right thing back."</li>
        <li>"I'm safe right now, but I've had thoughts that scare me, and I wanted someone to know."</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Say what you need</h2>
      <p>People guess badly. Make it easy for them: "Just listen." "Check on me tomorrow." "Sit with me." "Help me find a number to call." Asking for a specific thing is not a burden — it's a map.</p>
      <h2>If it goes badly</h2>
      <p>Sometimes the first person freezes, or says the wrong thing, or isn't who you hoped. That is information about that conversation — not a verdict on you, and not a verdict on asking. Try the next door: call or text 988 any time, or call the California Peer-Run Warm Line at <a href="tel:+18556009276">1-855-600-WARM (9276)</a> — peers who expect exactly this call.</p>
      <h2>If someone tells you</h2>
      <p>Listen more than you talk. Believe them. Ask what they need. If you're worried they might act on it, ask directly — asking does not plant the idea. Stay, and help them call 988 if it comes to that. You don't have to be a professional. You have to be a person who stayed.</p>
      <p>And if tonight is the night you're deciding whether to say it at all: say it. Start with the <a href="/resources/tonight/">For tonight</a> page if you need the next hour first.</p>
    </div>
